---
# Static analysis for the public headers and the test tree, over the generated self-sufficiency units and the test
# sources the Clang-Tidy workflow's sources_regex names.
# A diagnostic raised inside a header is reported only if that header matches HeaderFilterRegex, which is why the
# filter carries test/include as well as include/xstd: a test header left out of it is analyzed and then silently
# discarded.
# test/.clang-tidy inherits this and drops the checks that only misfire on fixtures.
# bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters is off because both sites it reports are fixed by the interface: to_string(charT
# zero, charT one) is std::bitset::to_string's signature, and lexicographical_three_way compares two bitsets.
# bugprone-exception-escape is off because it fires on the small-vector column's defaulted default constructor, which
# cannot throw: std::is_nothrow_default_constructible_v holds for boost::container::small_vector, and the check reaches
# a throw only by walking Boost's vector base into allocation_command, a path default construction never takes. There
# is no narrower switch: the diagnostic lands on a header every column shares, and IgnoredExceptions does not match a
# throw found through an indirect call.
# bugprone-crtp-constructor-accessibility is off because the three adaptors are not pure CRTP bases: one named at its
# default trailing void is its own container, so the constructors the check wants protected are the ones it is built
# from, and every container inherits them through `using base_type::base_type`, which keeps the base's access.
# Checks is a folded scalar, so a '#' inside it is literal text and not a comment: explanations go above the key.
Checks: >
  bugprone-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  misc-*,
  modernize-*,
  performance-*,
  portability-*,
  readability-*,
  -misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,
  -readability-avoid-nested-conditional-operator,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-named-parameter,
  -readability-simplify-boolean-expr,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-crtp-constructor-accessibility,
  -bugprone-exception-escape
WarningsAsErrors: '*'
HeaderFilterRegex: '(include/xstd|test/include)/.*'
FormatStyle: none
CheckOptions:
  # Prefer alternative tokens for logical operators.
  readability-operators-representation.BinaryOperators: 'not;and;or'
  # No Boost library ships IWYU pragmas, so nothing there is attributable to a header a consumer names.
  # Any project including a Boost library needs this line of its own.
  misc-include-cleaner.IgnoreHeaders: 'boost/.*'
  # A lambda already writes its return type after the parameter list, which is
  # the whole of what this check is for, so for a generic one its suggestion is
  # the vacuous "-> auto". A lambda whose deduced type is concrete is still
  # required to name it. clang-tidy 21 began transforming lambdas; 20 silently
  # accepts the option and does nothing with it, having said nothing about
  # lambdas in the first place.
  modernize-use-trailing-return-type.TransformLambdas: all_except_auto
  # Iterators are passed by value, as the standard library's own signatures take
  # them. The check measures cost by trivial copyability, and Boost's
  # function_input_iterator, the tests' C++17 iterator without a default
  # constructor, is not trivially copyable, so it would ask the iterator-pair
  # constructors, assign and insert for references the standard does not use.
  performance-unnecessary-value-param.AllowedTypes: '[Ii]terator(<|$)'
